From: =?UTF-8?q?Fran=C3=A7ois=20Roux?= <info@humanlearning.ch>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:05:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qdsp6: audioreach: Fail the ADSP readiness probe fast
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

audioreach_send_cmd_sync() waits 5 seconds for every command. That budget
is right for graph commands, which run against an ADSP that has already
answered and where a short timeout could truncate a legitimate slow
response mid-playback.

APM_CMD_GET_SPF_STATE is different. It is the readiness probe q6prm issues
before populating its children, it is the only command sent while the ADSP
may not be listening yet, and its caller already treats failure as
-EPROBE_DEFER and retries:

	q6prm_probe() -> if (!q6apm_is_adsp_ready()) return -EPROBE_DEFER;

On a Surface Pro 12in (X1P42100) booted at EL2 via slbounce, the ADSP is
*attached* rather than started -- it was brought up by the UEFI driver
before ExitBootServices, so the kernel skips the boot handshake that
normally precedes GPR traffic. The first readiness probe therefore always
misses and the second always succeeds. Exactly one timeout per boot, and
the full 5 seconds are spent waiting for a reply that will never come:

	qcom-apm gprsvc:service:2:1: CMD timeout for [1001021] opcode

The cost is not theoretical. The sound card only registers once the probe
gives up, so any service polling /proc/asound/cards blocks for the whole
timeout -- here it was the slowest unit of the entire boot.

Give that one probe a 1 second budget and let the existing retry do the
work. Measured on the machine above:

	                          before      after
	timeout logged at         8.68 s      5.70 s
	sound card registered     8.71 s      5.74 s
	card-waiting service      4.40 s      0.37 s
	total boot                8.21 s      6.74 s

Every other command keeps 5 * HZ.

Not sent upstream: the underlying issue is arguably that the attach path
should wait for the GPR service to come up rather than probing blind, and
that is a larger change than shortening one timeout.

Tool assistance, per Documentation/process/generated-content.rst: the
diff, the commit message and the measurements quoted above were produced in a
long interactive session with an AI coding assistant. The human signer directed
the work, challenged the analysis repeatedly -- several intermediate conclusions
were wrong and were retracted after measurement -- ran every test on the
machine, and reviewed the result. No specialised analysis tool (coccinelle,
sparse, smatch) was used; the verification was empirical, on the hardware
described.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: François Roux <info@humanlearning.ch>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/audioreach.c b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/audioreach.c
index e6e9eb2e8..742601bed 100644
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/audioreach.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/audioreach.c
@@ -599,11 +599,29 @@ int audioreach_send_cmd_sync(struct device *dev, gpr_device_t *gdev,
 	if (rc < 0)
 		goto err;
 
+	/*
+	 * APM_CMD_GET_SPF_STATE is the readiness probe q6prm issues before
+	 * populating its children; it is the only command sent while the ADSP
+	 * may not be listening yet, and its caller already treats failure as
+	 * -EPROBE_DEFER and retries. On a Surface Pro 12 booted at EL2, where
+	 * the ADSP is attached rather than started (so the driver skips the
+	 * boot handshake that normally precedes GPR traffic), the first probe
+	 * always misses and the second always succeeds -- costing the full
+	 * timeout, once, on every boot. That delay is the single slowest item
+	 * in userspace startup: the sound card appears only when it expires.
+	 *
+	 * Fail this one probe fast and let the retry do the work. Every other
+	 * command keeps the 5 s budget, since those run against an ADSP that
+	 * has already answered and a short timeout could truncate a legitimate
+	 * slow response mid-playback.
+	 */
+	unsigned long timeout = (hdr->opcode == APM_CMD_GET_SPF_STATE) ? HZ : 5 * HZ;
+
 	if (rsp_opcode)
 		rc = wait_event_timeout(*cmd_wait, (result->opcode == hdr->opcode) ||
-					(result->opcode == rsp_opcode),	5 * HZ);
+					(result->opcode == rsp_opcode),	timeout);
 	else
-		rc = wait_event_timeout(*cmd_wait, (result->opcode == hdr->opcode), 5 * HZ);
+		rc = wait_event_timeout(*cmd_wait, (result->opcode == hdr->opcode), timeout);
 
 	if (!rc) {
 		dev_err(dev, "CMD timeout for [%x] opcode\n", hdr->opcode);
